Fair — that was hand-wavy, and it's the kind of thing that's wrong in a subtle way if you don't pin it down. Here's the exact logic.

## The rule that makes it work

**The error travels in the return value. The data travels in pointer parameters. They never share a lane.**

A C function can only return one thing. We need two things to come back out of each recursive call: "did anything break?" and "how many black nodes on this path?" So the error code is the return value, and the black-height comes back through a pointer the caller hands in — same trick `prev` and `count` already use.

## Exact signature

```c
static int validate_subtree(const struct rbnode *n,
                            const char **prev,        /* in/out: last key seen */
                            size_t *count,            /* in/out: nodes seen */
                            size_t *black_height);    /* out: this subtree's height */
```

One new parameter compared to what's there now. Returns `RB_OK` (0) or the first broken rule's code.

## The logic, in order

```c
if (n == NULL) {
    *black_height = 1;                  /* the invisible black leaf (rule 3) */
    return RB_OK;
}

/* checks on THIS node, before looking at anything below it */
if (n->left  != NULL && n->left->parent  != n)  return RB_BAD_PARENT;
if (n->right != NULL && n->right->parent != n)  return RB_BAD_PARENT;
if (n->color == RB_RED && (is_red(n->left) || is_red(n->right)))
                                                 return RB_BAD_RED_RED;

/* LEFT side. If anything below is broken, stop here and pass it up. */
size_t left_bh = 0;
int rc = validate_subtree(n->left, prev, count, &left_bh);
if (rc != RB_OK) return rc;                    /* <-- the early abort */

/* this node, in sorted order */
if (*prev != NULL && strcmp(*prev, n->key) >= 0) return RB_BAD_ORDER;
*prev = n->key;
(*count)++;

/* RIGHT side. Same rule. */
size_t right_bh = 0;
rc = validate_subtree(n->right, prev, count, &right_bh);
if (rc != RB_OK) return rc;                    /* <-- the early abort */

/* Both sides came back clean. NOW compare them. */
if (left_bh != right_bh) return RB_BAD_BLACK_HEIGHT;

*black_height = left_bh + (n->color == RB_BLACK ? 1 : 0);
return RB_OK;
```

## Tracing your exact scenario: a mismatch 4 levels down on the left

Call chain: `rb_validate` → `vs(root)` → `vs(L1)` → `vs(L2)` → `vs(L3)` → `vs(L4)`, where `L4`'s two children have different black-heights.

| Where | What happens |
|---|---|
| `vs(L4)` | Both children return OK. `left_bh != right_bh` → **`return 7`**. The `*black_height = ...` line at the bottom **never runs**. |
| `vs(L3)` | It called `vs(L4)` as its left child. Gets `rc = 7`. Hits `if (rc != RB_OK) return rc;` → **returns 7 immediately.** Its right-child call, its order check, its own height comparison — none of that code runs. |
| `vs(L2)` | Same: `rc = 7`, returns 7. |
| `vs(L1)` | Same. |
| `vs(root)` | Same. |
| `rb_validate` | `rc = 7`, returns 7. |

**Why it can't be overwritten:** `return` ends the function. There is no code path where a nonzero `rc` gets checked and then something *after* it runs. Every recursive call is immediately followed by the abort check, and the height comparison only happens after *both* calls have come back clean. So the first failure found — in walk order — is the one reported, every time.

**Why `left_bh = 0` before the call:** if the call fails, it may return without ever writing to `*black_height`. We never read it in that case (we return first), but initializing it keeps `-Werror` from complaining about a possibly-uninitialized variable under `-O1`.

**Why the parent and red-red checks come before recursing:** so a broken node reports *its own* problem before we descend into its children. Doesn't change correctness, but it means the shallowest problem is what you see in the failure message.

## `rb_validate` itself

```c
if (t->root != NULL) {
    if (t->root->parent != NULL)     return RB_BAD_ROOT_PARENT;
    if (t->root->color  != RB_BLACK) return RB_BAD_ROOT_COLOR;
}

const char *prev = NULL;
size_t count = 0;
size_t bh = 0;
int rc = validate_subtree(t->root, &prev, &count, &bh);
if (rc != RB_OK) return rc;
if (count != t->count) return RB_BAD_COUNT;
return RB_OK;
```

`bh` is received but not compared to anything at the top — the root's height is whatever it is. What matters is that every path *below* agreed with every other, and that's already been enforced at every node on the way up.

Let me put this exact logic into the plan so it's on record.

